    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "We're back on hold these truths. And I've got my friend and special guest representative, Brian Fitzpatrick. We serve on the intelligence committee together. I'll say a little bit about your background, of course, you're representative from Pennsylvania. And prior to that, you were an FBI agent and federal prosecutor for 14 years, served as national director for the FBI's campaign finance and election crimes enforcement program and a national supervisor for the FBI's public corruption unit at FBI headquarters. represents the first congressional district in Pennsylvania since 2016 election, so even serving since 2017. You're on hipcy, which is the Intel Committee on Ways and Means. And boy, we had a bunch of conversations today. I mean, where do we even begin? So we're here to talk about Pfizer, the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Act. We've done a podcast on this before with Chairman Turner and Darren Littlehood. And Brian, you couldn't make that one, but I really wanted you from your perspective because you were in the FBI. So you got a really solid perspective on both sides of this, you know, from like the civil liberties concerns we have as members of Congress. But also like the nuts and bolts of the actual program and what's true and what isn't. And there are many debates we've had in conference today. I made the comment like, I was offended because somebody's like, well, Danny, you just care about security more than you care about liberty. And I'm like, that kind of pisses me off when you say that. Because that's what I say to Democrats when they want to over-regulate all of our industries. You know, this could happen. So we need to regulate it. That's an insistence on security over liberty. But I might remind them that like, you know, part of the government's role in protecting our natural rights is to protect us from each other. And so there's security role there. And there's always gonna be a debate on how much security is appropriate. And what we're talking about, Fiza, there's just a ton of misinformation out there. That is, I think, driving us all a little crazy during this debate. What are some of the conversations you've had today? Where do you wanna begin on this?",
