    "turn_text": "offered an amendment to change that, which is still arguing against, which is kind of nuts. So that's what Pfizer is. For almost a year, it seems like there's been a working group trying to figure out what the best reforms are because there has been abuses of this system because those foreigners that we have warrants on, they talk to Americans sometimes. Now, it's important for people to realize that the analogy of a wiretap is probably the right analogy. And it's something people are used to. So you get a warrant and you wiretap somebody. Well, you're wiretapping them because you want to know who they talked to. and you're listening to those conversations. You never ignore the other side of the conversation, as you're in a BI, you're in a cop, but never ignore the other side of that conversation. The only difference between that concept and Pfizer is that in Pfizer, it's a searchable database, all of those conversations, because they're tech messages, they're emails. And so you have access to Osama bin Laden's inbox, and in that inbox, there are conversations. Now, just because you see those conversations doesn't mean you then see the inbox of the person who they have the conversation with. That is such an important point. I think a lot of people don't understand. And so when we query and you're querying a US person, and there's a lot of limitations on what kind of US persons you can query and why you're querying them, first of all. You're not searching their inbox. You're not getting their records. And that was said earlier in our own debates here behind closed doors. Some members think that's what's happening. Of course, it's not what's happening. The only thing that would pop up would be their",
