    "turn_text": "You know, there are searches that there are an expectation of privacy on US persons that implicate the Fourth Amendment, and then there are those that don't. Me searching for my lost sunglasses does not implicate the Fourth Amendment. Me going into your house and downloading your computer does. And the best way I can bring it home for your viewers, Dan, is on a local law enforcement level. Every police department has an evidence locker. That locker contains evidence that was lawfully ceased for showing to a court order. taken to its logical extreme, that would require, this new concept would require a warrant to walk back into that evidence locker. It's already being collected. And yet they can't even go in and view it without getting another warrant. There were 371 financial intelligence, I'm sorry, foreign intelligence surveillance court voters last year, 371. If you have a warrant requirement for a query that would magnify to probably about 200,000. Not 200,000 people by the way, 200,000 searches, 200,000 clicks of the enter button. There are 11, I believe, nine or 11 judges that rotate on and off the fisc. There's about 650 federal judges that are part of that rotation. And you'd have to have another 1500 Biden appointed federal court judges to just keep just purely from a math standpoint, not to mention that we're creating this brand new standard in US law of having law enforcement searches of their databases have a warrant requirement. It's insane on",
