    "turn_text": "these emails that I'm talking about. And the hypothetical that I'll lay out goes as follows. So Osama bin Laden. He's got a warrant on him because he's a foreign terrorist. And so we have access to his emails. There's some emails sent to him. Okay, now in the FISA process, you don't just like automatically see the emails. You do have to click through and there's prompts and things and it asks you why you're doing it and all this. Under a warrant process, which requires probable cause, you wouldn't even get a warrant to open up all of the emails sent to him if they come from US persons. There's another question that they don't answer to, which is like, so what's that FBI agent supposed to actually do? What if they don't know who that came from? That's not really clear in the law. So since they don't know if that email came from a US person, they're going to err on the side of, well, I'm not going to open it because I don't want to go to jail. And so now you're just not going to see it. And even if they did, even if they were willing to take the risk and take the, well, sit, there's probably a thousand emails sent to that one inbox. So wait, you got to get a warrant to open up every single email, half of which might be spam. But maybe one of them says, hey, thanks for the bomb making class. And so you have no idea, just looking at an inbox, like which one said, hey, I'd like to sell you some real estate or two, thanks for the bomb making class that went really well. We've got this great plan in Philadelphia. You actually will never be able to see it because the court will actually not approve a warrant based on simple association.",
