    "turn_text": "the abuses that are called out. It's important to contextualize what those abuses were, because people said, well, there was 300,000 illegal queries or something like that. It's some large number. Some of which were the really bad examples like I laid out earlier, but it's important to know for people that a lot of those examples are simple things like they queried an email. They don't know where the email came from. It's just part of your investigative process. That email is in a lawfully collected inbox. You want to know what else it's attached to or if it's talked to anybody else, turns out it's a US person. They then have to report that as a violation. It's not the sinister violation. Another one is, and a lot of them apparently were, simply the fact that they didn't narrow their searches enough. So they have a perfectly good reason for querying an identifier. When we query somebody, we say it could be identifier, it could be a number, it could be an email, it's an identifier. You know, what they're supposed to do when I think of the reforms we make is you have to query that within, you know, the Hezbollah collection set, not just everything. And if you do it, if you mistakenly or on purpose search the entire database as opposed to just like the Hezbollah database. That's a violation. But again, it's like, so I just want people to understand that. Is it the wrong thing to do? Yeah, we've decided it's the wrong thing to do. But it's also not as sinister as I think some people are making it out to",
